Setting up rented audio visual equipment for an outdoor event comes down to three things our crew controls on delivery day: power confirmed before we arrive, cable runs suited to the actual ground, and sound and lighting positioned around whatever wind and weather show up. Here is exactly how that setup happens.

Looking for which package fits your outdoor venue instead? See our [audio visual rental for outdoor events](/blog/audio-visual-rental-for-outdoor-events/) guide.

## Brief Overview

---

Malaga Event Gear's crew handles the full outdoor AV setup: confirming power, running cable for the real ground surface, weatherproofing the equipment, and testing everything before your event starts. Backed by experience going back to 1996, this guide walks through what happens on delivery day and the technical factors that differ from an indoor setup.

## Key Highlights

---

-   Power is confirmed with you before delivery day; we do not stock generators, so venue power access has to be known in advance.

-   Cable runs are adapted on site to the actual ground, whether that is grass, sand or decking.

-   Outdoor PA systems are positioned to project across open air, not to fill a room the way an indoor system does.

-   We do not rent tents or canopies: if rain is a real risk, a covered backup area needs to be arranged with your venue in advance.

-   The full system is tested by our crew before your event starts; you never set it up yourself.

## Power and Cabling: What Happens Before Equipment Even Arrives

Before we load the van, we confirm your venue's power access with you: how many outlets, where they are, and whether the circuit can handle a full sound and lighting rig. This matters because Malaga Event Gear does not stock generators; if a venue genuinely has no power access, that has to be solved separately, before booking, not discovered on the day.

Once on site, our crew runs cable suited to the actual ground: a villa garden usually means cable laid across grass or gravel, a beach club means routing around sand and foot traffic, and a rooftop or terrace means working around fixed furniture and shorter, more direct runs. Cable is routed away from guest paths and secured against trip hazards before anything is powered on.

<InlineCTA />

## Wind, Weather and How Outdoor Sound Behaves

Sound behaves differently outdoors than it does in a room. With no walls to reflect off, open air disperses audio further, so our PA systems are angled and positioned to project across the actual space rather than fill it the way an indoor system does. Wind changes where we place speaker stands and which microphones we use: a windy terrace calls for different mic choices than a sheltered garden, and stands are weighted and positioned to stay stable in gusts.

We do not rent tents or canopies. If rain is a genuine risk for your date, arrange a covered backup area with your venue in advance; our technicians assess conditions on site on the day and place equipment around whatever shelter is actually available.

## What Our Crew Checks on Delivery Day

Once the system is built and cabled, our crew runs a full test before your event starts: every speaker and microphone channel is checked individually, gain and levels are set for the actual outdoor conditions on the day, and any screen or projector is synced with the audio before the first guest arrives. On packages with a technician, that same person stays on site to monitor sound and adjust for changing conditions, wind picking up or a crowd filling in, during your event.

